    capstoneExercise: { title: "Custom Memory Allocators capstone program", type: "build-program", instructions: "Read an integer n and print n * 4 + 4. This is the core cost calculation of your Custom Memory Allocators capstone: n is the workload size and the output is the total cost your system must handle.", starterCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    if (!(std::cin >> n)) return 0;\n    // Implement the capstone cost calculation and print it.\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    if (!(std::cin >> n)) return 0;\n    std::cout << n * 4 + 4 << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", solutionExplanation: "The solution applies the capstone formula n * 4 + 4, so input 5 gives 24 and input 8 gives 36.", tests: [{ name: "Capstone example", stdin: "5", expectedStdout: "24\n", hidden: false }, { name: "Capstone hidden input", stdin: "8", expectedStdout: "36\n", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "The capstone for Custom Memory Allocators centres on one core calculation; the instructions state its formula exactly.", approach: "Read n, multiply by the stated weight, add the constant, and print the result.", structure: "A small main function is enough: read, compute, print." } },
    capstone: { title: "Custom Memory Allocators Capstone Project", description: "Build a working custom memory allocators system that demonstrates the techniques from this track, with clean code, tests, and documentation.", requirements: ["Implement the core functionality of the system using the techniques from both modules.", "Handle invalid input and error cases gracefully instead of crashing.", "Include at least three automated tests that verify the core behaviour.", "Document how to build and run the project and explain the key design decisions."], rubric: ["Core functionality works correctly for the main use cases.", "Error handling covers invalid input and edge cases.", "Tests pass and cover the most important behaviour.", "Code is organised, readable, and documented."] }
