      { id: "cpp-security-m2-l5", slug: "cpp-security-m2-l5", title: "Building a Secure Component", concept: "This topic is a technique used in real C++ systems to make code clearer, faster, or safer, and it builds directly on the fundamentals from the first module of this track. In Security in C++, this matters because C++ gives direct access to memory, so security is a property of the code itself, not something added later. Getting it right here avoids bugs that only show up under real load or with real data.", syntax: "auto result = compute(input); // adapt the pattern to your own types", exampleCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nint building_a_secure_componentScore(int base, int bonus) {\n    return base * 3 + bonus * 4;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    std::cout << building_a_secure_componentScore(40, 3) << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", exampleLanguage: "cpp", mistake: "Using = instead of == inside an if condition.", bug: { prompt: "The program should print the sum of 3 and 4, but it prints -1. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "int a = 3, b = 4;\nint sum = a - b;\nstd::cout << sum << \"\\n\";", fixedCode: "int a = 3, b = 4;\nint sum = a + b;\nstd::cout << sum << \"\\n\";", explanation: "The code subtracts instead of adding, so it computes 3 - 4 = -1; use + for a sum." }, exercise: { title: "Practise Building a Secure Component", type: "build-program", instructions: "Read two integers a and b and print a * 3 + b * 4. This is the weighted combination used when scoring a building a secure component design: the first input weighs 3 and the second weighs 4.", starterCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int a, b;\n    if (!(std::cin >> a >> b)) return 0;\n    // Combine a and b and print the result.\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int a, b;\n    if (!(std::cin >> a >> b)) return 0;\n    std::cout << a * 3 + b * 4 << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", solutionExplanation: "The solution reads the input, applies the formula from the instructions exactly, and prints the result, so the visible input 4 3 gives 24 and the hidden input 7 2 gives 29.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "4 3", expectedStdout: "24\n", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "7 2", expectedStdout: "29\n", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Building a Secure Component is about combining inputs with fixed weights, and the formula in the instructions states exactly which weights to use.", approach: "Read the input values first, then apply the formula step by step and print only the final number.", structure: "Keep main small: read input, compute the value in one expression, and print it followed by a newline." } } }
