      { id: "cpp-build-game-engine-m2-l1", slug: "cpp-build-game-engine-m2-l1", title: "Input System", concept: "This topic is a technique used in real C++ systems to make code clearer, faster, or safer, and it builds directly on the fundamentals from the first module of this track. In Building a Game Engine in C++, this matters because an engine is a framework other people build games on, so its interfaces matter as much as its speed. Getting it right here avoids bugs that only show up under real load or with real data.", syntax: "auto result = compute(input); // adapt the pattern to your own types", exampleCode: "#include <iostream>\n\ntemplate <typename T>\nT input_systemCombine(T a, T b) { return a + b; }\n\nint main() {\n    std::cout << input_systemCombine(34, 7) << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", exampleLanguage: "cpp", mistake: "Forgetting to include the right header for a standard library type.", bug: { prompt: "The program should print the average of 5 and 6 as 5.5, but it prints 5. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "int a = 5, b = 6;\nint avg = (a + b) / 2;\nstd::cout << avg << \"\\n\";", fixedCode: "int a = 5, b = 6;\ndouble avg = (a + b) / 2.0;\nstd::cout << avg << \"\\n\";", explanation: "Integer division truncates the fractional part, so (a + b) / 2 discards it; dividing by 2.0 keeps the fraction." }, exercise: { title: "Practise Input System", type: "write-code", instructions: "Read two integers a and b and print a * 7 + b * 4. This is the weighted combination used when scoring a input system design: the first input weighs 7 and the second weighs 4.", starterCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int a, b;\n    if (!(std::cin >> a >> b)) return 0;\n    // Combine a and b and print the result.\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int a, b;\n    if (!(std::cin >> a >> b)) return 0;\n    std::cout << a * 7 + b * 4 << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", solutionExplanation: "The solution reads the input, applies the formula from the instructions exactly, and prints the result, so the visible input 4 3 gives 40 and the hidden input 7 2 gives 57.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "4 3", expectedStdout: "40\n", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "7 2", expectedStdout: "57\n", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Input System is about combining inputs with fixed weights, and the formula in the instructions states exactly which weights to use.", approach: "Read the input values first, then apply the formula step by step and print only the final number.", structure: "Keep main small: read input, compute the value in one expression, and print it followed by a newline." } } },
      { id: "cpp-build-game-engine-m2-l2", slug: "cpp-build-game-engine-m2-l2", title: "Asset Loading", concept: "This topic is a technique used in real C++ systems to make code clearer, faster, or safer, and it builds directly on the fundamentals from the first module of this track. In Building a Game Engine in C++, this matters because an engine is a framework other people build games on, so its interfaces matter as much as its speed. Getting it right here avoids bugs that only show up under real load or with real data.", syntax: "auto result = compute(input); // adapt the pattern to your own types", exampleCode: "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector<int> asset_loadingValues = {48, 54, 60};\n    int total = std::accumulate(asset_loadingValues.begin(), asset_loadingValues.end(), 0);\n    std::cout << total << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", exampleLanguage: "cpp", mistake: "Capturing a local variable by reference in a lambda that outlives it.", bug: { prompt: "The program should print each element once, but it skips the last one. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "std::vector<int> v = {2, 4, 6};\nfor (size_t i = 0; i + 1 < v.size(); i++) {\n  std::cout << v[i] << \"\\n\";\n}", fixedCode: "std::vector<int> v = {2, 4, 6};\nfor (size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); i++) {\n  std::cout << v[i] << \"\\n\";\n}", explanation: "The condition i + 1 < size stops one element early; use i < size to visit every element." }, exercise: { title: "Practise Asset Loading", type: "build-program", instructions: "Read an integer n and print n*(n+1)/2 + 6. This triangular-number total is the number of pairs to check in a asset loading scenario with n items, plus a fixed overhead of 6.", starterCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    if (!(std::cin >> n)) return 0;\n    // Print the triangular number for n plus the constant.\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    if (!(std::cin >> n)) return 0;\n    std::cout << n * (n + 1) / 2 + 6 << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", solutionExplanation: "The solution reads the input, applies the formula from the instructions exactly, and prints the result, so the visible input 6 gives 27 and the hidden input 10 gives 61.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "6", expectedStdout: "27\n", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "10", expectedStdout: "61\n", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Asset Loading is about combining inputs with fixed weights, and the formula in the instructions states exactly which weights to use.", approach: "Read the input values first, then apply the formula step by step and print only the final number.", structure: "Keep main small: read input, compute the value in one expression, and print it followed by a newline." } } },
      { id: "cpp-build-game-engine-m2-l3", slug: "cpp-build-game-engine-m2-l3", title: "Scene Management", concept: "This topic is a technique used in real C++ systems to make code clearer, faster, or safer, and it builds directly on the fundamentals from the first module of this track. In Building a Game Engine in C++, this matters because an engine is a framework other people build games on, so its interfaces matter as much as its speed. Getting it right here avoids bugs that only show up under real load or with real data.", syntax: "auto result = compute(input); // adapt the pattern to your own types", exampleCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nstruct Scene_managementCounter {\n    int value = 22;\n    void add(int n) { value += n; }\n    int get() const { return value; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    Scene_managementCounter counter;\n    counter.add(5);\n    counter.add(4);\n    std::cout << counter.get() << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", exampleLanguage: "cpp", mistake: "Forgetting to check whether a pointer is null before dereferencing it.", bug: { prompt: "The program should double a value through a reference, but the caller's value does not change. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "void doubleIt(int x) { x *= 2; }\nint main() {\n  int v = 4;\n  doubleIt(v);\n  std::cout << v << \"\\n\";\n}", fixedCode: "void doubleIt(int& x) { x *= 2; }\nint main() {\n  int v = 4;\n  doubleIt(v);\n  std::cout << v << \"\\n\";\n}", explanation: "The parameter is passed by value, so the function doubles a copy; passing by reference lets the change reach the caller." }, exercise: { title: "Check your understanding of Scene Management", type: "multiple-choice", instructions: "Which statement about Scene Management is correct?", starterCode: "", solution: "This topic is a technique used in real C++ systems to make code clearer, faster, or safer, and it builds directly on the fundamentals from the first module of this track.", solutionExplanation: "This topic is a technique used in real C++ systems to make code clearer, faster, or safer, and it builds directly on the fundamentals from the first module of this track.", tests: [{ name: "Correct answer", expectedContains: "This topic is a technique used in real C++ systems to make code clearer, faster, or safer, and it builds directly on the fundamentals from the first module of this track.", hidden: false }], hintNotes: { concept: "Recall the definition of Scene Management from the lesson: it is a real technique with a specific purpose, not a limitation or a paid feature.", approach: "Rule out the options that claim the feature is unavailable, paid, or single-use, then check the remaining option against the lesson.", structure: "The correct option restates the key fact from the concept section of the lesson." }, options: ["Scene Management can only be used once in a program", "Scene Management requires a separate paid licence to use", "Scene Management was removed from C++ in the latest version", "This topic is a technique used in real C++ systems to make code clearer, faster, or safer, and it builds directly on the fundamentals from the first module of this track."], answerIndex: 3 } },
      { id: "cpp-build-game-engine-m2-l4", slug: "cpp-build-game-engine-m2-l4", title: "Scripting Hooks", concept: "This topic is a technique used in real C++ systems to make code clearer, faster, or safer, and it builds directly on the fundamentals from the first module of this track. In Building a Game Engine in C++, this matters because an engine is a framework other people build games on, so its interfaces matter as much as its speed. Getting it right here avoids bugs that only show up under real load or with real data.", syntax: "auto result = compute(input); // adapt the pattern to your own types", exampleCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int scripting_hooksTotal = 0;\n    for (int i = 1; i <= 6; ++i) {\n        scripting_hooksTotal += i * 4;\n    }\n    std::cout << scripting_hooksTotal << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", exampleLanguage: "cpp", mistake: "Forgetting to free memory allocated with new.", bug: { prompt: "The program should print the numbers 1 to 5, but it prints only 1 to 4. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "for (int i = 1; i < 5; i++) {\n  std::cout << i << \"\\n\";\n}", fixedCode: "for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {\n  std::cout << i << \"\\n\";\n}", explanation: "The loop stops at 4 because the condition is i < 5; using i <= 5 includes 5." }, exercise: { title: "Practise Scripting Hooks", type: "build-function", instructions: "Read an integer n from standard input and print n * 4 + 4. Use it to model a small scripting hooks calculation for Building a Game Engine in C++: the input is the workload size and the output is the computed cost.", starterCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    if (!(std::cin >> n)) return 0;\n    // Compute the result for n and print it.\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    if (!(std::cin >> n)) return 0;\n    std::cout << n * 4 + 4 << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", solutionExplanation: "The solution reads the input, applies the formula from the instructions exactly, and prints the result, so the visible input 5 gives 24 and the hidden input 9 gives 40.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "5", expectedStdout: "24\n", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "9", expectedStdout: "40\n", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Scripting Hooks is about combining inputs with fixed weights, and the formula in the instructions states exactly which weights to use.", approach: "Read the input values first, then apply the formula step by step and print only the final number.", structure: "Keep main small: read input, compute the value in one expression, and print it followed by a newline." } } },
      { id: "cpp-build-game-engine-m2-l5", slug: "cpp-build-game-engine-m2-l5", title: "Engine Capstone Integration", concept: "A capstone project combines everything in the track into one working system, and it is judged on whether it runs, is tested, and is easy to explain. In Building a Game Engine in C++, this matters because an engine is a framework other people build games on, so its interfaces matter as much as its speed. Getting it right here avoids bugs that only show up under real load or with real data.", syntax: "auto result = compute(input); // adapt the pattern to your own types", exampleCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nint engine_capstone_integrationScore(int base, int bonus) {\n    return base * 3 + bonus * 4;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    std::cout << engine_capstone_integrationScore(10, 3) << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", exampleLanguage: "cpp", mistake: "Using = instead of == inside an if condition.", bug: { prompt: "The program should print the sum of 3 and 4, but it prints -1. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "int a = 3, b = 4;\nint sum = a - b;\nstd::cout << sum << \"\\n\";", fixedCode: "int a = 3, b = 4;\nint sum = a + b;\nstd::cout << sum << \"\\n\";", explanation: "The code subtracts instead of adding, so it computes 3 - 4 = -1; use + for a sum." }, exercise: { title: "Practise Engine Capstone Integration", type: "build-program", instructions: "Read two integers a and b and print a * 3 + b * 4. This is the weighted combination used when scoring a engine capstone integration design: the first input weighs 3 and the second weighs 4.", starterCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int a, b;\n    if (!(std::cin >> a >> b)) return 0;\n    // Combine a and b and print the result.\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int a, b;\n    if (!(std::cin >> a >> b)) return 0;\n    std::cout << a * 3 + b * 4 << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", solutionExplanation: "The solution reads the input, applies the formula from the instructions exactly, and prints the result, so the visible input 4 3 gives 24 and the hidden input 7 2 gives 29.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "4 3", expectedStdout: "24\n", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "7 2", expectedStdout: "29\n", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Engine Capstone Integration is about combining inputs with fixed weights, and the formula in the instructions states exactly which weights to use.", approach: "Read the input values first, then apply the formula step by step and print only the final number.", structure: "Keep main small: read input, compute the value in one expression, and print it followed by a newline." } } }
