      { id: "cpp-build-raytracer-m1-l1", slug: "cpp-build-raytracer-m1-l1", title: "Ray Fundamentals", concept: "A ray tracer follows rays from the camera through each pixel into the scene, and the colour of a pixel is decided by the first object the ray hits. In Building a Ray Tracer in C++, this matters because a ray tracer simulates light one ray at a time, so a beautiful image is just a lot of simple geometry repeated. Getting it right here avoids bugs that only show up under real load or with real data.", syntax: "Ray ray{origin, direction}; hit = scene.intersect(ray);", exampleCode: "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector<int> ray_fundamentalsValues = {12, 17, 22};\n    int total = std::accumulate(ray_fundamentalsValues.begin(), ray_fundamentalsValues.end(), 0);\n    std::cout << total << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", exampleLanguage: "cpp", mistake: "Forgetting to free memory allocated with new.", bug: { prompt: "The program should print the numbers 1 to 5, but it prints only 1 to 4. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "for (int i = 1; i < 5; i++) {\n  std::cout << i << \"\\n\";\n}", fixedCode: "for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {\n  std::cout << i << \"\\n\";\n}", explanation: "The loop stops at 4 because the condition is i < 5; using i <= 5 includes 5." }, exercise: { title: "Practise Ray Fundamentals", type: "write-code", instructions: "Read an integer n from standard input and print n * 5 + 4. Use it to model a small ray fundamentals calculation for Building a Ray Tracer in C++: the input is the workload size and the output is the computed cost.", starterCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    if (!(std::cin >> n)) return 0;\n    // Compute the result for n and print it.\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    if (!(std::cin >> n)) return 0;\n    std::cout << n * 5 + 4 << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", solutionExplanation: "The solution reads the input, applies the formula from the instructions exactly, and prints the result, so the visible input 5 gives 29 and the hidden input 9 gives 49.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "5", expectedStdout: "29\n", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "9", expectedStdout: "49\n", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Ray Fundamentals is about combining inputs with fixed weights, and the formula in the instructions states exactly which weights to use.", approach: "Read the input values first, then apply the formula step by step and print only the final number.", structure: "Keep main small: read input, compute the value in one expression, and print it followed by a newline." } } },
      { id: "cpp-build-raytracer-m1-l2", slug: "cpp-build-raytracer-m1-l2", title: "Camera Model", concept: "A camera model maps pixel coordinates to rays using the field of view and the camera position, so changing the view only changes the rays. In Building a Ray Tracer in C++, this matters because a ray tracer simulates light one ray at a time, so a beautiful image is just a lot of simple geometry repeated. Getting it right here avoids bugs that only show up under real load or with real data.", syntax: "auto result = compute(input); // adapt the pattern to your own types", exampleCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nstruct Camera_modelCounter {\n    int value = 26;\n    void add(int n) { value += n; }\n    int get() const { return value; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    Camera_modelCounter counter;\n    counter.add(4);\n    counter.add(4);\n    std::cout << counter.get() << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", exampleLanguage: "cpp", mistake: "Using = instead of == inside an if condition.", bug: { prompt: "The program should print the sum of 3 and 4, but it prints -1. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "int a = 3, b = 4;\nint sum = a - b;\nstd::cout << sum << \"\\n\";", fixedCode: "int a = 3, b = 4;\nint sum = a + b;\nstd::cout << sum << \"\\n\";", explanation: "The code subtracts instead of adding, so it computes 3 - 4 = -1; use + for a sum." }, exercise: { title: "Practise Camera Model", type: "build-function", instructions: "Read two integers a and b and print a * 4 + b * 4. This is the weighted combination used when scoring a camera model design: the first input weighs 4 and the second weighs 4.", starterCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int a, b;\n    if (!(std::cin >> a >> b)) return 0;\n    // Combine a and b and print the result.\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int a, b;\n    if (!(std::cin >> a >> b)) return 0;\n    std::cout << a * 4 + b * 4 << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", solutionExplanation: "The solution reads the input, applies the formula from the instructions exactly, and prints the result, so the visible input 4 3 gives 28 and the hidden input 7 2 gives 36.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "4 3", expectedStdout: "28\n", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "7 2", expectedStdout: "36\n", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Camera Model is about combining inputs with fixed weights, and the formula in the instructions states exactly which weights to use.", approach: "Read the input values first, then apply the formula step by step and print only the final number.", structure: "Keep main small: read input, compute the value in one expression, and print it followed by a newline." } } },
      { id: "cpp-build-raytracer-m1-l3", slug: "cpp-build-raytracer-m1-l3", title: "Sphere Intersection", concept: "This topic is a technique used in real C++ systems to make code clearer, faster, or safer, and it builds directly on the fundamentals from the first module of this track. In Building a Ray Tracer in C++, this matters because a ray tracer simulates light one ray at a time, so a beautiful image is just a lot of simple geometry repeated. Getting it right here avoids bugs that only show up under real load or with real data.", syntax: "auto result = compute(input); // adapt the pattern to your own types", exampleCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int sphere_intersectionTotal = 0;\n    for (int i = 1; i <= 5; ++i) {\n        sphere_intersectionTotal += i * 4;\n    }\n    std::cout << sphere_intersectionTotal << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", exampleLanguage: "cpp", mistake: "Passing a large object by value instead of by reference.", bug: { prompt: "The program should print 10, but it prints 5. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "int value = 5;\nvalue = value;\nstd::cout << value << \"\\n\";", fixedCode: "int value = 5;\nvalue = value * 2;\nstd::cout << value << \"\\n\";", explanation: "The assignment does nothing because it assigns the variable to itself; the doubling step was missing." }, exercise: { title: "Check your understanding of Sphere Intersection", type: "multiple-choice", instructions: "Which statement about Sphere Intersection is correct?", starterCode: "", solution: "This topic is a technique used in real C++ systems to make code clearer, faster, or safer, and it builds directly on the fundamentals from the first module of this track.", solutionExplanation: "This topic is a technique used in real C++ systems to make code clearer, faster, or safer, and it builds directly on the fundamentals from the first module of this track.", tests: [{ name: "Correct answer", expectedContains: "This topic is a technique used in real C++ systems to make code clearer, faster, or safer, and it builds directly on the fundamentals from the first module of this track.", hidden: false }], hintNotes: { concept: "Recall the definition of Sphere Intersection from the lesson: it is a real technique with a specific purpose, not a limitation or a paid feature.", approach: "Rule out the options that claim the feature is unavailable, paid, or single-use, then check the remaining option against the lesson.", structure: "The correct option restates the key fact from the concept section of the lesson." }, options: ["Sphere Intersection can only be used once in a program", "Sphere Intersection requires a separate paid licence to use", "This topic is a technique used in real C++ systems to make code clearer, faster, or safer, and it builds directly on the fundamentals from the first module of this track.", "Sphere Intersection was removed from C++ in the latest version"], answerIndex: 2 } },
      { id: "cpp-build-raytracer-m1-l4", slug: "cpp-build-raytracer-m1-l4", title: "Shading and Lighting", concept: "Shading computes the colour of a surface from its material and the lights in the scene, usually with a diffuse term and a specular highlight. In Building a Ray Tracer in C++, this matters because a ray tracer simulates light one ray at a time, so a beautiful image is just a lot of simple geometry repeated. Getting it right here avoids bugs that only show up under real load or with real data.", syntax: "color = diffuse * light + specular;", exampleCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nint shading_and_lightingScore(int base, int bonus) {\n    return base * 7 + bonus * 4;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    std::cout << shading_and_lightingScore(14, 7) << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", exampleLanguage: "cpp", mistake: "Forgetting a virtual destructor in a base class.", bug: { prompt: "The program should print the number of elements in a vector, but it crashes. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "std::vector<int> v = {1, 2, 3};\nstd::cout << v[3] << \"\\n\";", fixedCode: "std::vector<int> v = {1, 2, 3};\nstd::cout << v.size() << \"\\n\";", explanation: "Index 3 is past the end of a 3-element vector, which is undefined behaviour; use size() to count the elements." }, exercise: { title: "Practise Shading and Lighting", type: "fix-bug", instructions: "The starter code prints n*n + 7*n but should print n*n + 7*n + 4. Fix the bug.", starterCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    if (!(std::cin >> n)) return 0;\n    std::cout << n * n + 7 * n << \"\\n\";  // BUG: the constant term is missing\n    return 0;\n}", solution: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    if (!(std::cin >> n)) return 0;\n    std::cout << n * n + 7 * n + 4 << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", solutionExplanation: "The solution reads the input, applies the formula from the instructions exactly, and prints the result, so the visible input 4 gives 48 and the hidden input 7 gives 102.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "4", expectedStdout: "48\n", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "7", expectedStdout: "102\n", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Shading and Lighting is about combining inputs with fixed weights, and the formula in the instructions states exactly which weights to use.", approach: "Read the input values first, then apply the formula step by step and print only the final number.", structure: "Keep main small: read input, compute the value in one expression, and print it followed by a newline." } } },
      { id: "cpp-build-raytracer-m1-l5", slug: "cpp-build-raytracer-m1-l5", title: "Shadows", concept: "A shadow ray is cast from a surface point toward each light, and if anything blocks the ray the point is in shadow for that light. In Building a Ray Tracer in C++, this matters because a ray tracer simulates light one ray at a time, so a beautiful image is just a lot of simple geometry repeated. Getting it right here avoids bugs that only show up under real load or with real data.", syntax: "auto result = compute(input); // adapt the pattern to your own types", exampleCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    auto shadowsCompute = [](int x) { return x * 6 + 4; };\n    std::cout << shadowsCompute(28) << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", exampleLanguage: "cpp", mistake: "Using an iterator after the container was modified.", bug: { prompt: "The program should print the larger of two numbers, but it prints the smaller one. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "int a = 7, b = 9;\nint best = (a < b) ? a : b;\nstd::cout << best << \"\\n\";", fixedCode: "int a = 7, b = 9;\nint best = (a > b) ? a : b;\nstd::cout << best << \"\\n\";", explanation: "The comparison is the wrong way round, so the ternary picks the smaller value; use a > b to pick the larger one." }, exercise: { title: "Check your understanding of Shadows", type: "multiple-choice", instructions: "Which statement about Shadows is correct?", starterCode: "", solution: "A shadow ray is cast from a surface point toward each light, and if anything blocks the ray the point is in shadow for that light.", solutionExplanation: "A shadow ray is cast from a surface point toward each light, and if anything blocks the ray the point is in shadow for that light.", tests: [{ name: "Correct answer", expectedContains: "A shadow ray is cast from a surface point toward each light, and if anything blocks the ray the point is in shadow for that light.", hidden: false }], hintNotes: { concept: "Recall the definition of Shadows from the lesson: it is a real technique with a specific purpose, not a limitation or a paid feature.", approach: "Rule out the options that claim the feature is unavailable, paid, or single-use, then check the remaining option against the lesson.", structure: "The correct option restates the key fact from the concept section of the lesson." }, options: ["A shadow ray is cast from a surface point toward each light, and if anything blocks the ray the point is in shadow for that light.", "Shadows can only be used once in a program", "Shadows requires a separate paid licence to use", "Shadows was removed from C++ in the latest version"], answerIndex: 0 } }
