      { id: "cpp-build-raytracer-m2-l1", slug: "cpp-build-raytracer-m2-l1", title: "Reflections", concept: "Reflection is a program's ability to inspect its own types and members at run time, which C++ supports in limited form through RTTI and typeid. In Building a Ray Tracer in C++, this matters because a ray tracer simulates light one ray at a time, so a beautiful image is just a lot of simple geometry repeated. Getting it right here avoids bugs that only show up under real load or with real data.", syntax: "const std::type_info& t = typeid(object);", exampleCode: "#include <iostream>\n\ntemplate <typename T>\nT reflectionsCombine(T a, T b) { return a + b; }\n\nint main() {\n    std::cout << reflectionsCombine(42, 5) << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", exampleLanguage: "cpp", mistake: "Forgetting to include the right header for a standard library type.", bug: { prompt: "The program should print the average of 5 and 6 as 5.5, but it prints 5. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "int a = 5, b = 6;\nint avg = (a + b) / 2;\nstd::cout << avg << \"\\n\";", fixedCode: "int a = 5, b = 6;\ndouble avg = (a + b) / 2.0;\nstd::cout << avg << \"\\n\";", explanation: "Integer division truncates the fractional part, so (a + b) / 2 discards it; dividing by 2.0 keeps the fraction." }, exercise: { title: "Practise Reflections", type: "write-code", instructions: "Read two integers a and b and print a * 5 + b * 4. This is the weighted combination used when scoring a reflections design: the first input weighs 5 and the second weighs 4.", starterCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int a, b;\n    if (!(std::cin >> a >> b)) return 0;\n    // Combine a and b and print the result.\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int a, b;\n    if (!(std::cin >> a >> b)) return 0;\n    std::cout << a * 5 + b * 4 << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", solutionExplanation: "The solution reads the input, applies the formula from the instructions exactly, and prints the result, so the visible input 4 3 gives 32 and the hidden input 7 2 gives 43.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "4 3", expectedStdout: "32\n", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "7 2", expectedStdout: "43\n", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Reflections is about combining inputs with fixed weights, and the formula in the instructions states exactly which weights to use.", approach: "Read the input values first, then apply the formula step by step and print only the final number.", structure: "Keep main small: read input, compute the value in one expression, and print it followed by a newline." } } },
      { id: "cpp-build-raytracer-m2-l2", slug: "cpp-build-raytracer-m2-l2", title: "Refraction", concept: "This topic is a technique used in real C++ systems to make code clearer, faster, or safer, and it builds directly on the fundamentals from the first module of this track. In Building a Ray Tracer in C++, this matters because a ray tracer simulates light one ray at a time, so a beautiful image is just a lot of simple geometry repeated. Getting it right here avoids bugs that only show up under real load or with real data.", syntax: "auto result = compute(input); // adapt the pattern to your own types", exampleCode: "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector<int> refractionValues = {16, 20, 24};\n    int total = std::accumulate(refractionValues.begin(), refractionValues.end(), 0);\n    std::cout << total << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", exampleLanguage: "cpp", mistake: "Capturing a local variable by reference in a lambda that outlives it.", bug: { prompt: "The program should print each element once, but it skips the last one. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "std::vector<int> v = {2, 4, 6};\nfor (size_t i = 0; i + 1 < v.size(); i++) {\n  std::cout << v[i] << \"\\n\";\n}", fixedCode: "std::vector<int> v = {2, 4, 6};\nfor (size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); i++) {\n  std::cout << v[i] << \"\\n\";\n}", explanation: "The condition i + 1 < size stops one element early; use i < size to visit every element." }, exercise: { title: "Practise Refraction", type: "build-program", instructions: "Read an integer n and print n*(n+1)/2 + 4. This triangular-number total is the number of pairs to check in a refraction scenario with n items, plus a fixed overhead of 4.", starterCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    if (!(std::cin >> n)) return 0;\n    // Print the triangular number for n plus the constant.\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    if (!(std::cin >> n)) return 0;\n    std::cout << n * (n + 1) / 2 + 4 << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", solutionExplanation: "The solution reads the input, applies the formula from the instructions exactly, and prints the result, so the visible input 6 gives 25 and the hidden input 10 gives 59.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "6", expectedStdout: "25\n", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "10", expectedStdout: "59\n", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Refraction is about combining inputs with fixed weights, and the formula in the instructions states exactly which weights to use.", approach: "Read the input values first, then apply the formula step by step and print only the final number.", structure: "Keep main small: read input, compute the value in one expression, and print it followed by a newline." } } },
      { id: "cpp-build-raytracer-m2-l3", slug: "cpp-build-raytracer-m2-l3", title: "Anti-Aliasing", concept: "Anti-aliasing traces several rays per pixel and averages the results, which smooths the jagged edges that a single sample per pixel produces. In Building a Ray Tracer in C++, this matters because a ray tracer simulates light one ray at a time, so a beautiful image is just a lot of simple geometry repeated. Getting it right here avoids bugs that only show up under real load or with real data.", syntax: "auto result = compute(input); // adapt the pattern to your own types", exampleCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nstruct Anti_aliasingCounter {\n    int value = 30;\n    void add(int n) { value += n; }\n    int get() const { return value; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    Anti_aliasingCounter counter;\n    counter.add(3);\n    counter.add(4);\n    std::cout << counter.get() << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", exampleLanguage: "cpp", mistake: "Forgetting to check whether a pointer is null before dereferencing it.", bug: { prompt: "The program should double a value through a reference, but the caller's value does not change. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "void doubleIt(int x) { x *= 2; }\nint main() {\n  int v = 4;\n  doubleIt(v);\n  std::cout << v << \"\\n\";\n}", fixedCode: "void doubleIt(int& x) { x *= 2; }\nint main() {\n  int v = 4;\n  doubleIt(v);\n  std::cout << v << \"\\n\";\n}", explanation: "The parameter is passed by value, so the function doubles a copy; passing by reference lets the change reach the caller." }, exercise: { title: "Check your understanding of Anti-Aliasing", type: "multiple-choice", instructions: "Which statement about Anti-Aliasing is correct?", starterCode: "", solution: "Anti-aliasing traces several rays per pixel and averages the results, which smooths the jagged edges that a single sample per pixel produces.", solutionExplanation: "Anti-aliasing traces several rays per pixel and averages the results, which smooths the jagged edges that a single sample per pixel produces.", tests: [{ name: "Correct answer", expectedContains: "Anti-aliasing traces several rays per pixel and averages the results, which smooths the jagged edges that a single sample per pixel produces.", hidden: false }], hintNotes: { concept: "Recall the definition of Anti-Aliasing from the lesson: it is a real technique with a specific purpose, not a limitation or a paid feature.", approach: "Rule out the options that claim the feature is unavailable, paid, or single-use, then check the remaining option against the lesson.", structure: "The correct option restates the key fact from the concept section of the lesson." }, options: ["Anti-Aliasing can only be used once in a program", "Anti-Aliasing requires a separate paid licence to use", "Anti-Aliasing was removed from C++ in the latest version", "Anti-aliasing traces several rays per pixel and averages the results, which smooths the jagged edges that a single sample per pixel produces."], answerIndex: 3 } },
      { id: "cpp-build-raytracer-m2-l4", slug: "cpp-build-raytracer-m2-l4", title: "Multithreading the Renderer", concept: "This topic is a technique used in real C++ systems to make code clearer, faster, or safer, and it builds directly on the fundamentals from the first module of this track. In Building a Ray Tracer in C++, this matters because a ray tracer simulates light one ray at a time, so a beautiful image is just a lot of simple geometry repeated. Getting it right here avoids bugs that only show up under real load or with real data.", syntax: "auto result = compute(input); // adapt the pattern to your own types", exampleCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int multithreading_the_rendererTotal = 0;\n    for (int i = 1; i <= 9; ++i) {\n        multithreading_the_rendererTotal += i * 4;\n    }\n    std::cout << multithreading_the_rendererTotal << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", exampleLanguage: "cpp", mistake: "Forgetting to free memory allocated with new.", bug: { prompt: "The program should print the numbers 1 to 5, but it prints only 1 to 4. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "for (int i = 1; i < 5; i++) {\n  std::cout << i << \"\\n\";\n}", fixedCode: "for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {\n  std::cout << i << \"\\n\";\n}", explanation: "The loop stops at 4 because the condition is i < 5; using i <= 5 includes 5." }, exercise: { title: "Practise Multithreading the Renderer", type: "build-function", instructions: "Read an integer n from standard input and print n * 7 + 4. Use it to model a small multithreading the renderer calculation for Building a Ray Tracer in C++: the input is the workload size and the output is the computed cost.", starterCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    if (!(std::cin >> n)) return 0;\n    // Compute the result for n and print it.\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    if (!(std::cin >> n)) return 0;\n    std::cout << n * 7 + 4 << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", solutionExplanation: "The solution reads the input, applies the formula from the instructions exactly, and prints the result, so the visible input 5 gives 39 and the hidden input 9 gives 67.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "5", expectedStdout: "39\n", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "9", expectedStdout: "67\n", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Multithreading the Renderer is about combining inputs with fixed weights, and the formula in the instructions states exactly which weights to use.", approach: "Read the input values first, then apply the formula step by step and print only the final number.", structure: "Keep main small: read input, compute the value in one expression, and print it followed by a newline." } } },
      { id: "cpp-build-raytracer-m2-l5", slug: "cpp-build-raytracer-m2-l5", title: "Ray Tracer Capstone Integration", concept: "A ray tracer follows rays from the camera through each pixel into the scene, and the colour of a pixel is decided by the first object the ray hits. In Building a Ray Tracer in C++, this matters because a ray tracer simulates light one ray at a time, so a beautiful image is just a lot of simple geometry repeated. Getting it right here avoids bugs that only show up under real load or with real data.", syntax: "Ray ray{origin, direction}; hit = scene.intersect(ray);", exampleCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nint ray_tracer_capstone_integrationScore(int base, int bonus) {\n    return base * 6 + bonus * 4;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    std::cout << ray_tracer_capstone_integrationScore(18, 6) << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", exampleLanguage: "cpp", mistake: "Using = instead of == inside an if condition.", bug: { prompt: "The program should print the sum of 3 and 4, but it prints -1. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "int a = 3, b = 4;\nint sum = a - b;\nstd::cout << sum << \"\\n\";", fixedCode: "int a = 3, b = 4;\nint sum = a + b;\nstd::cout << sum << \"\\n\";", explanation: "The code subtracts instead of adding, so it computes 3 - 4 = -1; use + for a sum." }, exercise: { title: "Practise Ray Tracer Capstone Integration", type: "build-program", instructions: "Read two integers a and b and print a * 6 + b * 4. This is the weighted combination used when scoring a ray tracer capstone integration design: the first input weighs 6 and the second weighs 4.", starterCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int a, b;\n    if (!(std::cin >> a >> b)) return 0;\n    // Combine a and b and print the result.\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int a, b;\n    if (!(std::cin >> a >> b)) return 0;\n    std::cout << a * 6 + b * 4 << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", solutionExplanation: "The solution reads the input, applies the formula from the instructions exactly, and prints the result, so the visible input 4 3 gives 36 and the hidden input 7 2 gives 50.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "4 3", expectedStdout: "36\n", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "7 2", expectedStdout: "50\n", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Ray Tracer Capstone Integration is about combining inputs with fixed weights, and the formula in the instructions states exactly which weights to use.", approach: "Read the input values first, then apply the formula step by step and print only the final number.", structure: "Keep main small: read input, compute the value in one expression, and print it followed by a newline." } } }
