      { id: "cpp-build-trading-system-m2", title: "Building the Trading System", level: "advanced", lessons: [
      { id: "cpp-build-trading-system-m2-l1", slug: "cpp-build-trading-system-m2-l1", title: "Risk Checks", concept: "A pre-trade risk check verifies limits such as maximum order size before an order reaches the market, because a bad order can lose money in milliseconds. In Building a Trading System in C++, this matters because a trading system must be both correct and fast, because a wrong order and a slow order both lose money. Getting it right here avoids bugs that only show up under real load or with real data.", syntax: "auto result = compute(input); // adapt the pattern to your own types", exampleCode: "#include <iostream>\n\ntemplate <typename T>\nT risk_checksCombine(T a, T b) { return a + b; }\n\nint main() {\n    std::cout << risk_checksCombine(16, 4) << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", exampleLanguage: "cpp", mistake: "Forgetting to include the right header for a standard library type.", bug: { prompt: "The program should print the average of 5 and 6 as 5.5, but it prints 5. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "int a = 5, b = 6;\nint avg = (a + b) / 2;\nstd::cout << avg << \"\\n\";", fixedCode: "int a = 5, b = 6;\ndouble avg = (a + b) / 2.0;\nstd::cout << avg << \"\\n\";", explanation: "Integer division truncates the fractional part, so (a + b) / 2 discards it; dividing by 2.0 keeps the fraction." }, exercise: { title: "Practise Risk Checks", type: "write-code", instructions: "Read two integers a and b and print a * 4 + b * 8. This is the weighted combination used when scoring a risk checks design: the first input weighs 4 and the second weighs 8.", starterCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int a, b;\n    if (!(std::cin >> a >> b)) return 0;\n    // Combine a and b and print the result.\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int a, b;\n    if (!(std::cin >> a >> b)) return 0;\n    std::cout << a * 4 + b * 8 << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", solutionExplanation: "The solution reads the input, applies the formula from the instructions exactly, and prints the result, so the visible input 4 3 gives 40 and the hidden input 7 2 gives 44.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "4 3", expectedStdout: "40\n", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "7 2", expectedStdout: "44\n", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Risk Checks is about combining inputs with fixed weights, and the formula in the instructions states exactly which weights to use.", approach: "Read the input values first, then apply the formula step by step and print only the final number.", structure: "Keep main small: read input, compute the value in one expression, and print it followed by a newline." } } },
      { id: "cpp-build-trading-system-m2-l2", slug: "cpp-build-trading-system-m2-l2", title: "Latency Optimisation", concept: "Latency in trading is measured in microseconds, so the hot path avoids allocation, locking, and system calls. In Building a Trading System in C++, this matters because a trading system must be both correct and fast, because a wrong order and a slow order both lose money. Getting it right here avoids bugs that only show up under real load or with real data.", syntax: "auto result = compute(input); // adapt the pattern to your own types", exampleCode: "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector<int> latency_optimisationValues = {30, 33, 36};\n    int total = std::accumulate(latency_optimisationValues.begin(), latency_optimisationValues.end(), 0);\n    std::cout << total << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", exampleLanguage: "cpp", mistake: "Capturing a local variable by reference in a lambda that outlives it.", bug: { prompt: "The program should print each element once, but it skips the last one. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "std::vector<int> v = {2, 4, 6};\nfor (size_t i = 0; i + 1 < v.size(); i++) {\n  std::cout << v[i] << \"\\n\";\n}", fixedCode: "std::vector<int> v = {2, 4, 6};\nfor (size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); i++) {\n  std::cout << v[i] << \"\\n\";\n}", explanation: "The condition i + 1 < size stops one element early; use i < size to visit every element." }, exercise: { title: "Practise Latency Optimisation", type: "build-program", instructions: "Read an integer n and print n*(n+1)/2 + 3. This triangular-number total is the number of pairs to check in a latency optimisation scenario with n items, plus a fixed overhead of 3.", starterCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    if (!(std::cin >> n)) return 0;\n    // Print the triangular number for n plus the constant.\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    if (!(std::cin >> n)) return 0;\n    std::cout << n * (n + 1) / 2 + 3 << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", solutionExplanation: "The solution reads the input, applies the formula from the instructions exactly, and prints the result, so the visible input 6 gives 24 and the hidden input 10 gives 58.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "6", expectedStdout: "24\n", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "10", expectedStdout: "58\n", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Latency Optimisation is about combining inputs with fixed weights, and the formula in the instructions states exactly which weights to use.", approach: "Read the input values first, then apply the formula step by step and print only the final number.", structure: "Keep main small: read input, compute the value in one expression, and print it followed by a newline." } } },
      { id: "cpp-build-trading-system-m2-l3", slug: "cpp-build-trading-system-m2-l3", title: "Persistence", concept: "This topic is a technique used in real C++ systems to make code clearer, faster, or safer, and it builds directly on the fundamentals from the first module of this track. In Building a Trading System in C++, this matters because a trading system must be both correct and fast, because a wrong order and a slow order both lose money. Getting it right here avoids bugs that only show up under real load or with real data.", syntax: "auto result = compute(input); // adapt the pattern to your own types", exampleCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nstruct PersistenceCounter {\n    int value = 44;\n    void add(int n) { value += n; }\n    int get() const { return value; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    PersistenceCounter counter;\n    counter.add(7);\n    counter.add(8);\n    std::cout << counter.get() << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", exampleLanguage: "cpp", mistake: "Forgetting to check whether a pointer is null before dereferencing it.", bug: { prompt: "The program should double a value through a reference, but the caller's value does not change. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "void doubleIt(int x) { x *= 2; }\nint main() {\n  int v = 4;\n  doubleIt(v);\n  std::cout << v << \"\\n\";\n}", fixedCode: "void doubleIt(int& x) { x *= 2; }\nint main() {\n  int v = 4;\n  doubleIt(v);\n  std::cout << v << \"\\n\";\n}", explanation: "The parameter is passed by value, so the function doubles a copy; passing by reference lets the change reach the caller." }, exercise: { title: "Check your understanding of Persistence", type: "multiple-choice", instructions: "Which statement about Persistence is correct?", starterCode: "", solution: "This topic is a technique used in real C++ systems to make code clearer, faster, or safer, and it builds directly on the fundamentals from the first module of this track.", solutionExplanation: "This topic is a technique used in real C++ systems to make code clearer, faster, or safer, and it builds directly on the fundamentals from the first module of this track.", tests: [{ name: "Correct answer", expectedContains: "This topic is a technique used in real C++ systems to make code clearer, faster, or safer, and it builds directly on the fundamentals from the first module of this track.", hidden: false }], hintNotes: { concept: "Recall the definition of Persistence from the lesson: it is a real technique with a specific purpose, not a limitation or a paid feature.", approach: "Rule out the options that claim the feature is unavailable, paid, or single-use, then check the remaining option against the lesson.", structure: "The correct option restates the key fact from the concept section of the lesson." }, options: ["Persistence can only be used once in a program", "Persistence requires a separate paid licence to use", "Persistence was removed from C++ in the latest version", "This topic is a technique used in real C++ systems to make code clearer, faster, or safer, and it builds directly on the fundamentals from the first module of this track."], answerIndex: 3 } },
      { id: "cpp-build-trading-system-m2-l4", slug: "cpp-build-trading-system-m2-l4", title: "Backtesting", concept: "This topic is a technique used in real C++ systems to make code clearer, faster, or safer, and it builds directly on the fundamentals from the first module of this track. In Building a Trading System in C++, this matters because a trading system must be both correct and fast, because a wrong order and a slow order both lose money. Getting it right here avoids bugs that only show up under real load or with real data.", syntax: "auto result = compute(input); // adapt the pattern to your own types", exampleCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int backtestingTotal = 0;\n    for (int i = 1; i <= 8; ++i) {\n        backtestingTotal += i * 8;\n    }\n    std::cout << backtestingTotal << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", exampleLanguage: "cpp", mistake: "Forgetting to free memory allocated with new.", bug: { prompt: "The program should print the numbers 1 to 5, but it prints only 1 to 4. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "for (int i = 1; i < 5; i++) {\n  std::cout << i << \"\\n\";\n}", fixedCode: "for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {\n  std::cout << i << \"\\n\";\n}", explanation: "The loop stops at 4 because the condition is i < 5; using i <= 5 includes 5." }, exercise: { title: "Practise Backtesting", type: "build-function", instructions: "Read an integer n from standard input and print n * 6 + 8. Use it to model a small backtesting calculation for Building a Trading System in C++: the input is the workload size and the output is the computed cost.", starterCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    if (!(std::cin >> n)) return 0;\n    // Compute the result for n and print it.\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    if (!(std::cin >> n)) return 0;\n    std::cout << n * 6 + 8 << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", solutionExplanation: "The solution reads the input, applies the formula from the instructions exactly, and prints the result, so the visible input 5 gives 38 and the hidden input 9 gives 62.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "5", expectedStdout: "38\n", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "9", expectedStdout: "62\n", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Backtesting is about combining inputs with fixed weights, and the formula in the instructions states exactly which weights to use.", approach: "Read the input values first, then apply the formula step by step and print only the final number.", structure: "Keep main small: read input, compute the value in one expression, and print it followed by a newline." } } },
      { id: "cpp-build-trading-system-m2-l5", slug: "cpp-build-trading-system-m2-l5", title: "Trading System Capstone Integration", concept: "A capstone project combines everything in the track into one working system, and it is judged on whether it runs, is tested, and is easy to explain. In Building a Trading System in C++, this matters because a trading system must be both correct and fast, because a wrong order and a slow order both lose money. Getting it right here avoids bugs that only show up under real load or with real data.", syntax: "auto result = compute(input); // adapt the pattern to your own types", exampleCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nint trading_system_capstone_integrationScore(int base, int bonus) {\n    return base * 5 + bonus * 8;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    std::cout << trading_system_capstone_integrationScore(32, 5) << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", exampleLanguage: "cpp", mistake: "Using = instead of == inside an if condition.", bug: { prompt: "The program should print the sum of 3 and 4, but it prints -1. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "int a = 3, b = 4;\nint sum = a - b;\nstd::cout << sum << \"\\n\";", fixedCode: "int a = 3, b = 4;\nint sum = a + b;\nstd::cout << sum << \"\\n\";", explanation: "The code subtracts instead of adding, so it computes 3 - 4 = -1; use + for a sum." }, exercise: { title: "Practise Trading System Capstone Integration", type: "build-program", instructions: "Read two integers a and b and print a * 5 + b * 8. This is the weighted combination used when scoring a trading system capstone integration design: the first input weighs 5 and the second weighs 8.", starterCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int a, b;\n    if (!(std::cin >> a >> b)) return 0;\n    // Combine a and b and print the result.\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int a, b;\n    if (!(std::cin >> a >> b)) return 0;\n    std::cout << a * 5 + b * 8 << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", solutionExplanation: "The solution reads the input, applies the formula from the instructions exactly, and prints the result, so the visible input 4 3 gives 44 and the hidden input 7 2 gives 51.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "4 3", expectedStdout: "44\n", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "7 2", expectedStdout: "51\n", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Trading System Capstone Integration is about combining inputs with fixed weights, and the formula in the instructions states exactly which weights to use.", approach: "Read the input values first, then apply the formula step by step and print only the final number.", structure: "Keep main small: read input, compute the value in one expression, and print it followed by a newline." } } }
    ] }
