      { id: "cpp-build-interpreter-m1-l1", slug: "cpp-build-interpreter-m1-l1", title: "Interpreter Architecture", concept: "An interpreter executes a program directly from its tree representation instead of compiling it to machine code first. In Building an Interpreter in C++, this matters because an interpreter is a program that runs other programs, so it must understand the language it executes completely. Getting it right here avoids bugs that only show up under real load or with real data.", syntax: "Value result = eval(node, env);", exampleCode: "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector<int> interpreter_architectureValues = {43, 49, 55};\n    int total = std::accumulate(interpreter_architectureValues.begin(), interpreter_architectureValues.end(), 0);\n    std::cout << total << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", exampleLanguage: "cpp", mistake: "Forgetting to free memory allocated with new.", bug: { prompt: "The program should print the numbers 1 to 5, but it prints only 1 to 4. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "for (int i = 1; i < 5; i++) {\n  std::cout << i << \"\\n\";\n}", fixedCode: "for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {\n  std::cout << i << \"\\n\";\n}", explanation: "The loop stops at 4 because the condition is i < 5; using i <= 5 includes 5." }, exercise: { title: "Practise Interpreter Architecture", type: "write-code", instructions: "Read an integer n from standard input and print n * 6 + 4. Use it to model a small interpreter architecture calculation for Building an Interpreter in C++: the input is the workload size and the output is the computed cost.", starterCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    if (!(std::cin >> n)) return 0;\n    // Compute the result for n and print it.\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    if (!(std::cin >> n)) return 0;\n    std::cout << n * 6 + 4 << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", solutionExplanation: "The solution reads the input, applies the formula from the instructions exactly, and prints the result, so the visible input 5 gives 34 and the hidden input 9 gives 58.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "5", expectedStdout: "34\n", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "9", expectedStdout: "58\n", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Interpreter Architecture is about combining inputs with fixed weights, and the formula in the instructions states exactly which weights to use.", approach: "Read the input values first, then apply the formula step by step and print only the final number.", structure: "Keep main small: read input, compute the value in one expression, and print it followed by a newline." } } },
      { id: "cpp-build-interpreter-m1-l2", slug: "cpp-build-interpreter-m1-l2", title: "Lexer for a Language", concept: "A lexer scans source text and groups characters into tokens such as identifiers, numbers, and operators, which is the first stage of a compiler. In Building an Interpreter in C++, this matters because an interpreter is a program that runs other programs, so it must understand the language it executes completely. Getting it right here avoids bugs that only show up under real load or with real data.", syntax: "Token token = lexer.nextToken();", exampleCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nstruct Lexer_for_a_languageCounter {\n    int value = 17;\n    void add(int n) { value += n; }\n    int get() const { return value; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    Lexer_for_a_languageCounter counter;\n    counter.add(5);\n    counter.add(4);\n    std::cout << counter.get() << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", exampleLanguage: "cpp", mistake: "Using = instead of == inside an if condition.", bug: { prompt: "The program should print the sum of 3 and 4, but it prints -1. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "int a = 3, b = 4;\nint sum = a - b;\nstd::cout << sum << \"\\n\";", fixedCode: "int a = 3, b = 4;\nint sum = a + b;\nstd::cout << sum << \"\\n\";", explanation: "The code subtracts instead of adding, so it computes 3 - 4 = -1; use + for a sum." }, exercise: { title: "Practise Lexer for a Language", type: "build-function", instructions: "Read two integers a and b and print a * 5 + b * 4. This is the weighted combination used when scoring a lexer for a language design: the first input weighs 5 and the second weighs 4.", starterCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int a, b;\n    if (!(std::cin >> a >> b)) return 0;\n    // Combine a and b and print the result.\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int a, b;\n    if (!(std::cin >> a >> b)) return 0;\n    std::cout << a * 5 + b * 4 << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", solutionExplanation: "The solution reads the input, applies the formula from the instructions exactly, and prints the result, so the visible input 4 3 gives 32 and the hidden input 7 2 gives 43.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "4 3", expectedStdout: "32\n", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "7 2", expectedStdout: "43\n", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Lexer for a Language is about combining inputs with fixed weights, and the formula in the instructions states exactly which weights to use.", approach: "Read the input values first, then apply the formula step by step and print only the final number.", structure: "Keep main small: read input, compute the value in one expression, and print it followed by a newline." } } },
      { id: "cpp-build-interpreter-m1-l3", slug: "cpp-build-interpreter-m1-l3", title: "Parser for a Language", concept: "A parser takes tokens and builds a tree that reflects the grammar of the language, reporting a syntax error when the tokens do not fit. In Building an Interpreter in C++, this matters because an interpreter is a program that runs other programs, so it must understand the language it executes completely. Getting it right here avoids bugs that only show up under real load or with real data.", syntax: "auto ast = parser.parse(tokens);", exampleCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int parser_for_a_languageTotal = 0;\n    for (int i = 1; i <= 6; ++i) {\n        parser_for_a_languageTotal += i * 4;\n    }\n    std::cout << parser_for_a_languageTotal << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", exampleLanguage: "cpp", mistake: "Passing a large object by value instead of by reference.", bug: { prompt: "The program should print 10, but it prints 5. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "int value = 5;\nvalue = value;\nstd::cout << value << \"\\n\";", fixedCode: "int value = 5;\nvalue = value * 2;\nstd::cout << value << \"\\n\";", explanation: "The assignment does nothing because it assigns the variable to itself; the doubling step was missing." }, exercise: { title: "Check your understanding of Parser for a Language", type: "multiple-choice", instructions: "Which statement about Parser for a Language is correct?", starterCode: "", solution: "A parser takes tokens and builds a tree that reflects the grammar of the language, reporting a syntax error when the tokens do not fit.", solutionExplanation: "A parser takes tokens and builds a tree that reflects the grammar of the language, reporting a syntax error when the tokens do not fit.", tests: [{ name: "Correct answer", expectedContains: "A parser takes tokens and builds a tree that reflects the grammar of the language, reporting a syntax error when the tokens do not fit.", hidden: false }], hintNotes: { concept: "Recall the definition of Parser for a Language from the lesson: it is a real technique with a specific purpose, not a limitation or a paid feature.", approach: "Rule out the options that claim the feature is unavailable, paid, or single-use, then check the remaining option against the lesson.", structure: "The correct option restates the key fact from the concept section of the lesson." }, options: ["Parser for a Language can only be used once in a program", "Parser for a Language requires a separate paid licence to use", "A parser takes tokens and builds a tree that reflects the grammar of the language, reporting a syntax error when the tokens do not fit.", "Parser for a Language was removed from C++ in the latest version"], answerIndex: 2 } },
      { id: "cpp-build-interpreter-m1-l4", slug: "cpp-build-interpreter-m1-l4", title: "Evaluating Expressions", concept: "Evaluating an expression walks the syntax tree and computes a value for each node, combining child values according to the operator. In Building an Interpreter in C++, this matters because an interpreter is a program that runs other programs, so it must understand the language it executes completely. Getting it right here avoids bugs that only show up under real load or with real data.", syntax: "auto result = compute(input); // adapt the pattern to your own types", exampleCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nint evaluating_expressionsScore(int base, int bonus) {\n    return base * 3 + bonus * 4;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    std::cout << evaluating_expressionsScore(45, 3) << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", exampleLanguage: "cpp", mistake: "Forgetting a virtual destructor in a base class.", bug: { prompt: "The program should print the number of elements in a vector, but it crashes. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "std::vector<int> v = {1, 2, 3};\nstd::cout << v[3] << \"\\n\";", fixedCode: "std::vector<int> v = {1, 2, 3};\nstd::cout << v.size() << \"\\n\";", explanation: "Index 3 is past the end of a 3-element vector, which is undefined behaviour; use size() to count the elements." }, exercise: { title: "Practise Evaluating Expressions", type: "fix-bug", instructions: "The starter code prints n*n + 3*n but should print n*n + 3*n + 4. Fix the bug.", starterCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    if (!(std::cin >> n)) return 0;\n    std::cout << n * n + 3 * n << \"\\n\";  // BUG: the constant term is missing\n    return 0;\n}", solution: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    if (!(std::cin >> n)) return 0;\n    std::cout << n * n + 3 * n + 4 << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", solutionExplanation: "The solution reads the input, applies the formula from the instructions exactly, and prints the result, so the visible input 4 gives 32 and the hidden input 7 gives 74.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "4", expectedStdout: "32\n", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "7", expectedStdout: "74\n", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Evaluating Expressions is about combining inputs with fixed weights, and the formula in the instructions states exactly which weights to use.", approach: "Read the input values first, then apply the formula step by step and print only the final number.", structure: "Keep main small: read input, compute the value in one expression, and print it followed by a newline." } } },
      { id: "cpp-build-interpreter-m1-l5", slug: "cpp-build-interpreter-m1-l5", title: "Variables and Scope", concept: "A scope maps names to values, and nested scopes let inner blocks shadow names from outer blocks while the outer bindings stay intact. In Building an Interpreter in C++, this matters because an interpreter is a program that runs other programs, so it must understand the language it executes completely. Getting it right here avoids bugs that only show up under real load or with real data.", syntax: "auto result = compute(input); // adapt the pattern to your own types", exampleCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    auto variables_and_scopeCompute = [](int x) { return x * 7 + 4; };\n    std::cout << variables_and_scopeCompute(19) << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", exampleLanguage: "cpp", mistake: "Using an iterator after the container was modified.", bug: { prompt: "The program should print the larger of two numbers, but it prints the smaller one. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "int a = 7, b = 9;\nint best = (a < b) ? a : b;\nstd::cout << best << \"\\n\";", fixedCode: "int a = 7, b = 9;\nint best = (a > b) ? a : b;\nstd::cout << best << \"\\n\";", explanation: "The comparison is the wrong way round, so the ternary picks the smaller value; use a > b to pick the larger one." }, exercise: { title: "Check your understanding of Variables and Scope", type: "multiple-choice", instructions: "Which statement about Variables and Scope is correct?", starterCode: "", solution: "A scope maps names to values, and nested scopes let inner blocks shadow names from outer blocks while the outer bindings stay intact.", solutionExplanation: "A scope maps names to values, and nested scopes let inner blocks shadow names from outer blocks while the outer bindings stay intact.", tests: [{ name: "Correct answer", expectedContains: "A scope maps names to values, and nested scopes let inner blocks shadow names from outer blocks while the outer bindings stay intact.", hidden: false }], hintNotes: { concept: "Recall the definition of Variables and Scope from the lesson: it is a real technique with a specific purpose, not a limitation or a paid feature.", approach: "Rule out the options that claim the feature is unavailable, paid, or single-use, then check the remaining option against the lesson.", structure: "The correct option restates the key fact from the concept section of the lesson." }, options: ["A scope maps names to values, and nested scopes let inner blocks shadow names from outer blocks while the outer bindings stay intact.", "Variables and Scope can only be used once in a program", "Variables and Scope requires a separate paid licence to use", "Variables and Scope was removed from C++ in the latest version"], answerIndex: 0 } }
