    capstoneExercise: { title: "Building a Thread Pool in C++ capstone program", type: "build-program", instructions: "Read an integer n and print n * 4 + 3. This is the core cost calculation of your Building a Thread Pool in C++ capstone: n is the workload size and the output is the total cost your system must handle.", starterCode: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    if (!(std::cin >> n)) return 0;\n    // Implement the capstone cost calculation and print it.\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    if (!(std::cin >> n)) return 0;\n    std::cout << n * 4 + 3 << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}", solutionExplanation: "The solution applies the capstone formula n * 4 + 3, so input 5 gives 23 and input 8 gives 35.", tests: [{ name: "Capstone example", stdin: "5", expectedStdout: "23\n", hidden: false }, { name: "Capstone hidden input", stdin: "8", expectedStdout: "35\n", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "The capstone for Building a Thread Pool in C++ centres on one core calculation; the instructions state its formula exactly.", approach: "Read n, multiply by the stated weight, add the constant, and print the result.", structure: "A small main function is enough: read, compute, print." } },
